{"name":"Budding Bloom","hex":"#deeabd","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"budding","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Beginning to develop."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The development of buds on a plant.","The practice of uniting a single scion bud with rootstock or bark."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/budding"},{"term":"bloom","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A blossom; the flower of a plant; an expanded bud.","Flowers."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cause to blossom; to make flourish.","To bestow a bloom upon; to make blooming or radiant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bloom"}],"entries":[{"title":"Budding","blocks":[{"text":"Budding or blastogenesis is a type of asexual reproduction in which a new organism develops from an outgrowth or bud due to cell division at one particular site. For example, the small bulb-like projection coming out from the yeast cell is known as a bud. Since the reproduction is asexual, the newly created organism is a clone and, excepting mutations, is genetically identical to the parent organism. Organisms such as hydra use regenerative cells for reproduction in the process of budding."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/S_cerevisiae_under_DIC_microscopy.jpg/330px-S_cerevisiae_under_DIC_microscopy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Budding"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Hydra_oligactis.jpg/330px-Hydra_oligactis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Hydra_oligactis.jpg/330px-Hydra_oligactis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hydra with two buds","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHydra_oligactis.jpg"}],"sounds":{"budding":[{